                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "An interesting choices matter adventure game that has different endings/outcomes depending on your actions (mostly during the last part of the game). \r\n\r\nYou play as Neil Conrad, a CDI detective in 1800's Ghara, which is in a tense relationship with Dhora, its previous colony. While the political climate is getting more and more tense, you will have to solve the murder (and other things) of a political figure and find out who was behind his assassination and if their involvement reaches far more than just one person's fate. \r\n\r\nThe game has a very pleasant atmosphere: The 1800's Ghara is a fictional sci-fi world with flying cars and technological advances that most Gharians have never seen before (some more than others). \r\n\r\nThe soundtrack that is being played whilst you're sleuthing murders, broken glass and other things is pleasant, thrilling or anxious, depending on the situation or mood but after a while tends to get rather repetitive as you play the game for the second or even the third time.\r\n\r\nFor an indie game. \"Lacuna\" serves a game that cant truly change the outcome of its ending. And we don't get this feature in indie games that often. Alternative endings, interesting lore and lastly, being a sleuth detective in an actively changing world that is about to explode. The developers did a great job with what they had, creating an interesting story, characters, cute pixel graphics and a very good soundtrack that accompanies you on your way to detective work and some secrets as well.\r\n\r\n(A note for achievement hunters: If you're going for 100% completion, please be sure to jot down the endings that you've already gotten (e.g *That person died while they were trying to save *that person and so on), because the game doesn't register any endings that the player has gotten).",
